course: dictionary
lesson: 3556
title: Feminine: a word with a gender
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Feminine is an adjective, and it has more than one meaning. It can mean typical of, or thought to be suitable for, women or girls. In grammar, it also names a group, called a gender, that some languages sort their nouns and words into.
explain: Many languages, such as French, sort every noun into a feminine or a masculine group. «Une personne» is feminine even for a man, so the group belongs to the grammar word, not to the real person. Learn each noun with its little article, like «la» or «une», and you will remember its group.
explain: English does not sort nouns into feminine and masculine groups the way French does, but some word forms show a female: actress, lioness and waitress. When you meet a new word, read the words around it for clues, just as you would with any other word.
fact: Feminine means typical of, or thought to be suitable for, women or girls.
fact: Feminine is also a gender: a group that some languages put nouns and words into.
fact: Adding -e for feminine often makes the last consonant heard: «petit» becomes «petite», said "puh-TEET".
fact: Word endings give hints: «un chapeau» (a hat) is almost always masculine, and «une casquette» (a cap) is almost always feminine.
fact: «Une personne» is feminine even for a man, and «un bébé» is masculine even for a baby girl.
exercise: In a language lesson, what does «feminine» mean? | A word for something very old | A word for a very loud sound | *A group that some languages sort nouns into, like «une casquette»
exercise: «Une personne» is feminine even for a man. What does this show? | Feminine means happy | *Gender belongs to the grammar word, not to the real person | A man is always a woman in French
exercise: Which word ending is almost always feminine? | *-ette | -oux | -ier | -eau
test: In «un chapeau» and «une casquette», which word is feminine? | both of them | *une casquette | un chapeau | neither of them
test: With a feminine noun, «vert» becomes «verte». What happens to the sound in «une jupe verte»? | Nothing changes at all | *The t is now said, so it sounds like "VAIRT" | The word is said more quietly | The word becomes a noun
exam: Which sentence about feminine is true? | Feminine always means a boy | *Feminine can describe a gender group, like the «la» words in French | Feminine means a very loud sound | Feminine only appears in Spanish
